\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RPastPartMood
\item[* relevant examples:] \mbox{}\\
1a. (the man) PRO be killed by his enemies $\rightarrow$ (the man) killed by his 
enemies\\
1b. (the man) PRO have been killed by his enemies $\rightarrow$ (the man) 
killed by his enemies\\
2a. (all those) PRO go to the movies $\rightarrow$ (all those) gone 
to the movies
2b. (all those) PRO have gone to the movies $\rightarrow$ (all those) gone 
to the movies
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] ClauseTOsentence
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) RC\_
MoodDetermination
\item[* date of creation:] 25-MAY-1988 
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] Dutch Rmoodpastpart
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 7/12/88.\ \ \ \ \ author: Lisette Appelo
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] to make open `anterelative' clauses with a past participle; note 
that the name `anterelative' is retained, although English has `postrelatives'.
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:] one rule for 
passives (which have voice = passive and a passaux) and one rule for ergatives;
one rule for a retrospective reading, and one for a non-retrospective reading. 
In case the rest of the sentence blocks one of these two readings, it will be 
filtered out in rc\_retro (analytically speaking).
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\

\end{enumerate}
